<p>The bus takes twenty-two minutes. I know because I've counted. Fourteen stops, one transfer at Maple and 5th, then six more stops to the brick apartment building where Chloe lives on the third floor. Unit 3B. The buzzer is sticky and you have to hold it for three seconds before it catches. The elevator smells like old carpet and ambition.</p>

<p>She opens the door before I knock. She always does that. Hears me coming down the hallway, the specific weight of my footsteps, and has the door swinging open with her hip propped against the frame by the time I reach it.</p>

<<dialog "Chloe" "Hey, you.">>

<p>Two words. But when Chloe says them, they carry about forty different meanings. Today the meaning is: I have a plan and you're going to either love it or hate it and I'm going to enjoy watching either way.</p>

<p>She's wearing an oversized band tee, no bra, and cotton shorts that barely qualify as clothing. Her black hair is loose, falling over one shoulder in a dark curtain that smells like coconut when she hugs me. She pulls me inside by my shirt collar, which she does sometimes, this casual ownership gesture that I pretend doesn't make my stomach flip.</p>

<p>Her apartment is controlled chaos. Books stacked on every surface, not on shelves but in piles, organized by some system only she understands. Candles that have melted into abstract sculptures on the windowsill. A string of fairy lights tacked above her bed that she never turns off. Clothes draped over every chair. Her laptop open on the kitchen counter, paused on some video I can't make out from here.</p>

<p>We end up on her bed because we always end up on her bed. It's the gravitational center of her universe. King-sized, piled with pillows, sheets that never quite make it into a proper arrangement. I sit on the edge. She sits cross-legged in the middle, facing me, her knees touching my thigh. Her green eyes are doing that thing. That bright, mischievous, I-have-a-secret thing.</p>

<<dialog "Chloe" "So... I got you something.">>

<p>"Oh yeah?"</p>

<p>"Mmhmm." She reaches behind a pillow. Her arm disappears into the pillow mountain and comes back holding a small bundle of fabric. Pink. She unfolds it with both hands, pinching the waistband between her thumbs and forefingers, holding it up like a flag.</p>

<p>Panties.</p>

<p>Pink. Lacy. A scalloped edge along the waistband and these little sheer panels on the sides where the lace thins out enough to see skin through. A tiny bow at the front center, right where the waistband dips into a V. They're not cheap. They're the kind of thing you buy at a lingerie store, not a drugstore, the kind with tissue paper in the bag.</p>

<<dialog "Chloe" "Try these on. For me.">>

<p>My brain short-circuits. "What?"</p>

<p>"Try them on." She jiggles them. The lace catches the light from her fairy strings. "For me."</p>

<p>"Those are... Chloe, those are panties."</p>

<p>"Ten points for observation." She grins. "Come on. Just under your jeans. Nobody would know except us."</p>

<p>"Why?"</p>

<<dialog "Chloe" "Because I think you'd look cute.">>

<p>She says it like it's the most obvious thing in the world. Like wondering why the sky is blue. Because it is. Because you'd look cute. Simple as that.</p>

<p>My face is on fire. I can feel the heat crawling up from my neck, through my cheeks, to the tips of my ears. She's looking at me with her head tilted, chin slightly down, eyes looking up through her lashes. She knows exactly what that angle does. She's weaponized it. That look could end wars or start them depending on what she wanted.</p>

<p>I look at the panties again. The pink is soft. Not neon, not garish. A dusky rose that's almost gentle. The lace is delicate, the kind that would feel like a whisper against skin. I can see the quality of the fabric from here, something silky underneath the lace overlay, something that would slide and cling rather than bunch.</p>

<p>My mouth is dry.</p>

